WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.158
Can an active brain region reveal a thought?

00:00:03.158 --> 00:00:06.316
A colorful image can outshine its evidence.

00:00:06.316 --> 00:00:09.474
Poldrack examined reverse inference in 2006.

00:00:09.474 --> 00:00:12.632
Inferring a process from activity needs evidence.

00:00:12.632 --> 00:00:15.789
One region may participate in many tasks.

00:00:15.789 --> 00:00:22.105
Think of an orchestra working together.

00:00:22.105 --> 00:00:28.421
One instrument can belong to many pieces.

00:00:28.421 --> 00:00:34.737
Tasks, comparisons and patterns provide information.

00:00:34.737 --> 00:00:41.053
The lines are a functional teaching schematic.

00:00:41.053 --> 00:00:44.211
Activity alone is not enough for mind-reading.

00:00:44.211 --> 00:00:47.368
That does not make brain imaging valueless.

00:00:47.368 --> 00:00:50.526
Better inference needs more comparisons.

00:00:50.526 --> 00:00:56.842
Ask what participants actually did.

00:00:56.842 --> 00:01:03.158
Ask what condition the image was compared with.

00:01:03.158 --> 00:01:06.316
This artistic network is not a medical scan.

00:01:06.316 --> 00:01:09.474
A single switch cannot summarize a person.

00:01:09.474 --> 00:01:12.632
Pair a striking image with careful questions.

00:01:12.632 --> 00:01:15.789
Knowing the brain includes knowing evidential limits.
